xnu: MERLIN 0097 scribbler window 8 MB + drop the 0095 early-jump

Boot 27 (0094-fv + merlin_excl_pa=0x9f800000-0x800000) still needs the
wider default, and the 0095 early-jump turned out to be destructive:

1. merlin_excl_len default 0x280000 -> 0x800000.  The window now spans
   0x9f800000-0xa0000000, which contains every victim photographed so far
   (0x9f98a000 / 0x9f9ea000 boot 20, 0x9fa08000 boot 21, 0x9fae0000 boot
   27 - the last one sat 0x600000 past the old 0x9fa80000 end, which is
   exactly why 0094 kept panicking in zalloc_validate_element()).
2. Drop 0095's early jump.  pmap_next_page() jumped first_avail to the end
   of the window on its first call; first_avail is topOfKernelData
   (~0x824e4000), far below the window, so the jump discarded ~476 MB of
   usable RAM (0x824e4000-0xa0000000).  A packed vm_page array is indexed
   by physical page, so a hole can only be skipped by sacrificing
   everything below it - 0094's suspend-during-enumeration + withhold-at-
   release is the only affordable scheme.
3. Two unconditional one-line forensics prints so a boot photo shows the
   active window and how many pages the release loop actually withheld
   (0092's skip print never fires, 0095's jump print is gone).

--- a/osfmk/arm/pmap/pmap.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm/pmap/pmap.c
@@ -2632,15 +2632,20 @@
 	return pmap_next_page(pnum);
 }
 
